{
  "abstract": "A Lean proof that f(n, k), the least number such that the edges of every graph with n vertices and k edges can be partitioned into at most f(n, k) cliques, satisfies f(n, k) = n² B(k/n²) + o(n²) uniformly in 0 ≤ k ≤ n(n − 1)/2, for an explicit continuous piecewise-algebraic function B on [0, 1/2]. B(d) = d for d ≤ 1/4; above 1/4 it is the larger of two curves built from explicit dual certificates on split, mixed and two-head graphs. This provides an asymptotic resolution of Erdős Problem 1017, as posed by Erdős and as stated by Bloom, who asks for an estimate of f(n, k) for k > n²/4. The formula is new to our knowledge. The proof passes through the fractional relaxation and proves again, in Lean, the case of the fractional-to-integral transfer of Balogh, He, Krueger, Nguyen and Wigal that it needs.",
  "authors": [
    {
      "name": "Ryan Pettry"
    }
  ],
